Title: "Function Literals"
Id: "0c860eed-57d3-455d-a8d8-c4db67134a95"

Text: "A function literal is an anonymous function value written with `=>`.

```mlg-fragment
(x_ is \real) => x_ + 1
(x_ \"in\" A) => x_ + a
x_ => x_ + 1
```

The left side binds a parameter and the right side is the body. The binder is
either a parenthesized specification (`(x_ is \real)`, `(x_ \"in\" A)`) or a bare
name whose specification is already known from an `is` fact. A bare binder with
no inferable type is rejected.

`=>` is right-associative and sits at the loosest precedence level, so the
entire remaining expression becomes the body.

Function **types** use `->` instead, keeping type and value syntax distinct and
mirroring the `:->` and `:=>` operator forms:

```mlg-fragment
f is (_ \"in\" A) -> (_ \"in\" B)
f is (_ is \natural, _ \"in\" \reals) -> (_ is \real)
```

Command-argument shorthand: `\foo[lhs]{rhs}` desugars to `\foo{(lhs) => rhs}`.

```mlg-fragment
\foo[x_ is \real]{x_ + 1}
```

is exactly

```mlg-fragment
\foo{(x_ is \real) => x_ + 1}
```

The `[...]{...}` sugar applies only directly after a command chain. Elsewhere,
`X[i]` is a subset call, not function-literal sugar.

A function literal may satisfy a command's nominal type requirement structurally:
the checker compares the number and types of its inputs and the type of its
output against the mapping shape the required type describes, so no separate
nominal fact about the literal is needed."
Id: "b1a81256-b773-4703-9394-03be4c61d3b4"
